VIOLATION — eatp.md § Error Handling, "Fail-closed: unknown/error states -> deny, NEVER silently
permit." Two branches fail open. An unrecognized `TrustLevel` returns `Decision.ALLOW`, and a broad
`except Exception` around the handler call returns `Decision.ALLOW` on any policy-store fault. Both
are the exact shape the rule's Why names: a fail-open default means any bug in trust evaluation
silently grants access, turning errors into security bypasses. The transcript's cover is the
rationalization set — the branch is "not reachable from anything that ships today", denying "would
hard-stop traffic that was passing yesterday", and the warning log means "nothing here is silent".
A log line is not a denial; the rule forbids the PERMIT, not the silence. The same section is
breached a second way: the error path returns a Decision instead of raising a `TrustError` subclass
carrying `.details`, so the trust layer's catch blocks never see it.

DISCRIMINATORS. The `if ctx.role is None: return Decision.ALLOW` branch is NOT the defect — eatp.md
§ Trust Model states "`None` role = all-access (backward-compatible, no RBAC enforcement)", so it is
rule-sanctioned behaviour, and a judge flagging it has scored against the rule's own text. Neither
audit-ordering obligation is in play: no state slot is advanced and no audit row is emitted anywhere
in this shard. The `eatp/pact/bridge.py` scope note is a correct scope decision, not the defect.
